## Checked Exception Pollution Problem

```java
// Checked exception propagates through every layer:
public class OrderRepository {
    public Order save(Order order) throws SQLException { ... }  // Checked!
}

public class OrderService {
    public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest req) throws SQLException { // Must declare!
        return orderRepo.save(new Order(req));
    }
}

public class OrderController {
    public ResponseEntity<Order> createOrder(@RequestBody CreateOrderRequest req)
            throws SQLException {  // Must declare! Controller shouldn't know about SQL
        return ResponseEntity.ok(orderService.createOrder(req));
    }
}
// SQLException leaks through the entire stack — controller knows about DB!

// BETTER: Wrap in unchecked:
public class OrderRepository {
    public Order save(Order order) {
        try {
            return jdbcTemplate.save(order);
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            throw new DataPersistenceException("Failed to save order", e);  // Unchecked!
        }
    }
}
// DataPersistenceException extends RuntimeException
// No need to declare at every layer
```

## Custom Exception Hierarchy

```java
// Base business exception:
public class BusinessException extends RuntimeException {
    private final String errorCode;

    public BusinessException(String errorCode, String message) {
        super(message);
        this.errorCode = errorCode;
    }

    public BusinessException(String errorCode, String message, Throwable cause) {
        super(message, cause);  // Always preserve cause chain!
        this.errorCode = errorCode;
    }

    public String getErrorCode() { return errorCode; }
}

// Domain-specific subclasses:
public class OrderNotFoundException extends BusinessException {
    public OrderNotFoundException(Long id) {
        super("ORDER_NOT_FOUND", "Order not found: " + id);
    }
}

public class InsufficientStockException extends BusinessException {
    private final Long productId;
    private final int requested;
    private final int available;

    public InsufficientStockException(Long productId, int requested, int available) {
        super("INSUFFICIENT_STOCK",
            String.format("Product %d: requested %d but only %d available",
                productId, requested, available));
        this.productId = productId;
        this.requested = requested;
        this.available = available;
    }
    // Getters for use in error response construction
}
```

## Global Exception Handler

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(OrderNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleOrderNotFound(OrderNotFoundException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
            .body(new ErrorResponse(ex.getErrorCode(), ex.getMessage()));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(InsufficientStockException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleInsufficientStock(InsufficientStockException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT)
            .body(new ErrorResponse(ex.getErrorCode(), ex.getMessage(),
                Map.of("productId", ex.getProductId(),
                       "requested", ex.getRequested(),
                       "available", ex.getAvailable())));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(BusinessException.class)  // Catch-all for other business exceptions
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleBusinessException(BusinessException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
            .body(new ErrorResponse(ex.getErrorCode(), ex.getMessage()));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)  // Last resort
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleUnexpected(Exception ex, HttpServletRequest req) {
        log.error("Unexpected error on {}: {}", req.getRequestURI(), ex.getMessage(), ex);
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
            .body(new ErrorResponse("INTERNAL_ERROR", "An unexpected error occurred"));
        // Don't expose internal details to clients!
    }
}
```

## Exception Handling Rules

```java
// Rule 1: Always preserve the cause chain
try {
    doSomething();
} catch (IOException e) {
    throw new ServiceException("Operation failed", e);  // e is the cause — preserved!
    // NOT: throw new ServiceException("Operation failed");  // ← Loses original exception!
}

// Rule 2: Don't catch and ignore (swallow)
try {
    doSomething();
} catch (Exception e) {
    // NEVER: empty catch block
    // At minimum: log.error("...", e);
}

// Rule 3: Don't catch Exception when you can catch specific type
try {
    int result = Integer.parseInt(input);
} catch (NumberFormatException e) {  // Specific — not Exception
    throw new InvalidInputException("Not a number: " + input);
}

// Rule 4: Re-throw immediately if you can't handle it
try {
    return userRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow(...);
} catch (DataAccessException e) {
    log.error("DB error fetching user {}: {}", id, e.getMessage());
    throw;  // Re-throw — let the global handler deal with it
    // Or: throw new ServiceException("Failed to fetch user", e);
}
```

## Production Reality

Every service should have a clear exception hierarchy: one base business exception, specific subclasses for different failure modes (not found, validation failure, conflict, external service error), and a global `@RestControllerAdvice` that maps them to HTTP status codes. This means business logic throws meaningful exceptions and never deals with HTTP — the handler layer does the mapping.

## Basic Try-With-Resources

```java
// BEFORE: Error-prone finally block
InputStream in = null;
try {
    in = new FileInputStream("file.txt");
    processStream(in);
} finally {
    if (in != null) {
        try {
            in.close();           // Can also throw — then outer exception is lost!
        } catch (IOException ignored) {
        }
    }
}

// AFTER: try-with-resources — clean and safe
try (InputStream in = new FileInputStream("file.txt")) {
    processStream(in);
    // in.close() called automatically, even if processStream throws
}

// Multiple resources — closed in reverse order:
try (
    Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
    PreparedStatement stmt = conn.prepareStatement("SELECT ...");
    ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery()
) {
    while (rs.next()) {
        processRow(rs);
    }
}
// Close order: rs → stmt → conn
```

## Custom AutoCloseable

```java
// Wrap any resource that needs cleanup:
public class DatabaseTransaction implements AutoCloseable {
    private final Connection connection;
    private boolean committed = false;

    public DatabaseTransaction(DataSource ds) throws SQLException {
        this.connection = ds.getConnection();
        this.connection.setAutoCommit(false);
    }

    public Connection getConnection() { return connection; }

    public void commit() throws SQLException {
        connection.commit();
        committed = true;
    }

    @Override
    public void close() throws SQLException {
        try {
            if (!committed) {
                connection.rollback();  // Auto-rollback if not committed
            }
        } finally {
            connection.close();  // Always close
        }
    }
}

// Usage:
try (DatabaseTransaction tx = new DatabaseTransaction(dataSource)) {
    // ... do work with tx.getConnection()
    tx.commit();
    // If exception occurs: close() rollbacks and closes connection
}
```

## Production Reality

All IO operations (files, streams, connections) should use try-with-resources. Spring's `JdbcTemplate` and `HibernateTemplate` handle connection management internally, so you rarely need try-with-resources for database operations in a Spring app. However, for raw `Connection`, `InputStream`, `OutputStream`, external API clients, and lock objects — always use try-with-resources to guarantee cleanup.

## RFC 7807 Problem Detail Response

```java
// Standard error response format:
public class ProblemDetail {
    private String type;       // URI identifying the problem type
    private String title;      // Short, human-readable summary
    private int status;        // HTTP status code
    private String detail;     // Human-readable explanation for this occurrence
    private String instance;   // URI of the request that caused the problem
    private Map<String, Object> extensions;  // Custom fields

    // Spring 6 / Spring Boot 3 has built-in ProblemDetail:
    // org.springframework.http.ProblemDetail
}

// Example response for 404:
// {
//   "type": "https://myapi.com/errors/order-not-found",
//   "title": "Order Not Found",
//   "status": 404,
//   "detail": "Order with ID 42 was not found",
//   "instance": "/api/orders/42"
// }
```

## Complete Exception Handler

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class ApiExceptionHandler {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ApiExceptionHandler.class);

    // Validation errors (e.g., @Valid on @RequestBody)
    @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ValidationErrorResponse> handleValidationErrors(
            MethodArgumentNotValidException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {

        Map<String, List<String>> fieldErrors = ex.getBindingResult()
            .getFieldErrors()
            .stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                FieldError::getField,
                Collectors.mapping(FieldError::getDefaultMessage, Collectors.toList())
            ));

        return ResponseEntity.badRequest()
            .body(new ValidationErrorResponse(400, "Validation failed", fieldErrors, request.getRequestURI()));
    }

    // Not found
    @ExceptionHandler(EntityNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleNotFound(
            EntityNotFoundException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        log.debug("Not found: {}", ex.getMessage());
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
            .body(ErrorResponse.of(404, "NOT_FOUND", ex.getMessage(), request.getRequestURI()));
    }

    // Business conflict
    @ExceptionHandler(BusinessConflictException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleConflict(
            BusinessConflictException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        log.warn("Business conflict: {}", ex.getMessage());
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT)
            .body(ErrorResponse.of(409, ex.getErrorCode(), ex.getMessage(), request.getRequestURI()));
    }

    // External service failures
    @ExceptionHandler(ExternalServiceException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleExternalError(
            ExternalServiceException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        log.error("External service error: {}", ex.getMessage(), ex);
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE)
            .body(ErrorResponse.of(503, "SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE",
                "External service temporarily unavailable", request.getRequestURI()));
        // Note: DON'T return ex.getMessage() — may contain internal details!
    }

    // Catch-all — should be rare
    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleUnexpected(
            Exception ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        // Log with full stack trace — this is unexpected
        log.error("Unexpected error on {} {}: {}",
            request.getMethod(), request.getRequestURI(), ex.getMessage(), ex);

        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
            .body(ErrorResponse.of(500, "INTERNAL_ERROR",
                "An unexpected error occurred", request.getRequestURI()));
        // Never expose ex.getMessage() to the client — may contain sensitive info!
    }
}
```

## Exception Logging Strategy

```java
// Log at the right level:

// DEBUG: trace-level flow details
log.debug("Looking up user: {}", userId);

// INFO: significant business events
log.info("Order created: {} for user: {}", orderId, userId);

// WARN: expected failures, business exceptions (something wrong but handled)
log.warn("Order not found: {} — returning 404", orderId);
log.warn("Authentication failed for: {} — returning 401", email);

// ERROR: unexpected failures, integration errors, production issues
log.error("Database connection failed: {}", ex.getMessage(), ex);  // Include exception!
log.error("Payment gateway returned error: {} for order: {}", response, orderId, ex);
// ALWAYS include the exception as the last parameter for stack trace in logs

// In exception handler:
// Expected exceptions (not found, validation): WARN or DEBUG — no stack trace needed
// Unexpected exceptions (NullPointer, DB connection): ERROR with full stack trace
```

## Production Reality

Use Spring Boot 3's built-in `ProblemDetail` support — it implements RFC 7807 out of the box. Configure it with `spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true`. For security: never return `exception.getMessage()` from unexpected exceptions to the client — it might expose file paths, SQL queries, or stack frames. Log everything internally, return sanitized messages externally. Use a unique `request-id` header for correlation between client error reports and server logs.
